parts":[{"text":"போர்","lemma":"போர்","gloss":"war"}],"gloss":"war"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"கழல்","parts":[{"text":"கழல்","lemma":"கழல்","gloss":"warrior anklet"}],"gloss":"anklet"},{"surface":"புனை","parts":[{"text":"புனை","lemma":"புனை","gloss":"wear"}],"gloss":"wearing"},{"surface":"திருந்து","parts":[{"text":"திருந்து","lemma":"திருந்து","gloss":"perfected"}],"gloss":"perfected"},{"surface":"அடி","parts":[{"text":"அடி","lemma":"அடி","gloss":"feet"}],"gloss":"feet"},{"surface":"கடு","parts":[{"text":"கடு","lemma":"கடு","gloss":"swift"}],"gloss":"swift"},{"surface":"மான்","parts":[{"text":"மான்","lemma":"மான்","gloss":"horse"}],"gloss":"horse"},{"surface":"கிள்ளி","parts":[{"text":"கிள்ளி","lemma":"கிள்ளி","gloss":"Kiḷḷi"}],"gloss":"Kiḷḷi"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"நின்னை","parts":[{"text":"நின்னை","lemma":"நின்னை","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"வியக்குமிவ்","parts":[{"text":"வியக்குமிவ்","lemma":"வியக்குமிவ்","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"வுலகம்","parts":[{"text":"வுலகம்","lemma":"வுலகம்","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"அ·து","parts":[{"text":"அ·து","lemma":"அ·து","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true}],"as_printed":true},{"words":[{"surface":"என்னோ","parts":[{"text":"என்","lemma":"என்","gloss":"what"},{"text":"ஓ","lemma":"ஓ","gloss":"wonder clitic"}],"gloss":"what is it?"},{"surface":"பெரும","parts":[{"text":"பெரும","lemma":"பெருமான்","gloss":"great lord (vocative)"}],"gloss":"O great lord"},{"surface":"உரைத்திசின்","parts":[{"text":"உரை","lemma":"உரை","gloss":"tell"},{"text":"த்","lemma":"த்","gloss":"increment"},{"text":"திசின்","lemma":"திசின்","gloss":"second person particle"}],"gloss":"please tell"},{"surface":"எமக்கே","parts":[{"text":"எமக்கே","lemma":"எமக்கே","gloss":"to us indeed"}],"gloss":"to us indeed","split_unverified":true}]}],"literal":"You indeed, if seeing battle, conquering battle, those / army warding off, standing opposite, / with a body abiding with scars struck by swords, / are sweet to hear, but unpleasant to look at! / They indeed, if seeing you, because of turning their backs, / with a body not knowing any injury, / are sweet to look at, but unpleasant to hear! / Therefore, you too are sweet in one way, they too are sweet in one way; / what indeed are there that do not match? O victorious in war, / wearing heroic anklets, with perfect feet, Kiḷḷi of swift horses! / This world wonders at you; that, / what is it, O great lord? Please tell us!","translation":"When you see a battle, you conquer it, warding off their army and standing face-to-face. With a body covered in scars from sword-strikes, you are sweet to hear of, but unpleasant to look upon! When they see you, they turn their backs and flee. With bodies that have never known a wound, they are sweet to look upon, but unpleasant to hear of! Therefore, you are sweet in one way, and they are sweet in another. What is there that does not match? O Kiḷḷi of swift horses, with perfect feet adorned with heroic anklets, victorious in war! This world wonders at you. Why is that, O great lord? Please tell us!","modern":"நீ போரைக் கண்டால், அப்போரை வென்று, பகைவரின் படையைத் தடுத்து எதிர்த்து நிற்பாய். வாளால் ஏற்பட்ட வடுக்கள் நிறைந்த உடம்போடு இருப்பதால், உன்னைப் பற்றிப் புகழக் கேட்பதற்கு நீ இனியவன்; ஆனால் காண்பதற்கு அச்சம் தருவதால் கண்ணுக்கு இன்னாதவன்! அவர்களோ உன்னைக் கண்டால், முதுகு காட்டி ஓடுவதால், எவ்விதக் காயமும் படாத உடம்போடு இருக்கிறார்கள். அதனால் அவர்கள் காண்பதற்குக் கண்ணுக்கு இனியவர்கள்; ஆனால் அவர்களின் புகழைக் கேட்பதற்குச் செவிக்கு இன்னாதவர்கள்! அதனால் நீயும் ஒரு வகையில் இனியவன்; அவர்களும் ஒரு வகையில் இனியவர்கள். இதில் ஒன்றோடொன்று பொருந்தாதது என்ன இருக்கிறது? வெற்றி தரும் போரையும், வீரக்கழல் அணிந்த திருந்திய அடிகளையும், வேகமான குதிரைகளையும் உடைய கிள்ளியே! இவ்வுலகம் உன்னை வியந்து பாராட்டுகிறது. பெருமானே, அது ஏன் என்று எங்களுக்குச் சொல்வாயாக!","explanation":"The poet Kōṉāṭṭu Eṟiccilūr Māṭalaṉ Maturai Kumaṟaṉār addresses the Chola king Kaṭumāṉ Kiḷḷi in this poem. He uses a clever poetic contrast to praise the king's valor while mocking his enemies. The king stands and fights, bearing honorable scars from sword-strikes; thus, his fame is sweet to hear, but his scarred body is terrifying to look at. In contrast, his enemies flee immediately, keeping their bodies unblemished and beautiful to look at, but their cowardice is shameful to hear of. Both the king and his enemies are sweet in one way and unpleasant in another, making them a perfect, ironic match. This poem belongs to Vākai tiṇai (victory), specifically Arasa vākai, celebrating the king's military prowess.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}